\section{Scripting workflow}{

For work directly in R, use an isolated session and call the required steps.
A session is an ordinary R environment holding your data, plan, and results.
\code{\link[=mics_session]{mics_session()}} does not start Shiny or open a browser. An empty session is
enough to read and inspect a worksheet; calculation additionally requires
prepared data. Use \code{mics_session(hh = hh, hl = hl)} for data already prepared
in your workspace, or \code{\link[=prepare_mics_data]{prepare_mics_data()}} for raw survey files.
These interfaces also make the app's engine available for testing:
\itemize{
\item \code{\link[=mics_session]{mics_session()}} creates an isolated session.
\item \code{\link[=prepare_mics_data]{prepare_mics_data()}} runs the preparation script.
\item \code{\link[=read_mics_tabulation]{read_mics_tabulation()}} reads a worksheet's instructions.
\item \code{\link[=tabulate_mics_table]{tabulate_mics_table()}} calculates cell results.
\item \code{\link[=check_mics_table]{check_mics_table()}} checks consistency.
\item \code{\link[=pivot_mics_table]{pivot_mics_table()}} arranges results for review.
\item \code{\link[=create_excel_workbooks]{create_excel_workbooks()}} creates output workbooks.
\item \code{\link[=write_mics_table]{write_mics_table()}} writes the results.
\item \code{\link[=write_mics_footnotes]{write_mics_footnotes()}} writes table footnotes.
\item \code{\link[=read_previous_mics_table]{read_previous_mics_table()}} reads an earlier output.
\item \code{\link[=compare_mics_tables]{compare_mics_tables()}} compares two outputs.
}
See \link{offline-workflow} for a complete scripting example.
}

\section{Individual steps and testing}{

\code{\link[=tabulate_v]{tabulate_v()}} and \code{\link[=tabulate_h]{tabulate_h()}} calculate a loaded plan in a specific
direction; \code{\link[=calc_cells]{calc_cells()}} tests cells against explicit input data.
\code{\link[=tabulate_extra_table]{tabulate_extra_table()}} maps supplied values to the loaded plan.
\code{\link[=row_condition_f]{row_condition_f()}} parses row predicates.
\code{\link[=col_condition_f]{col_condition_f()}} parses column predicates.

\code{\link[=normalize_condition_text]{normalize_condition_text()}} normalizes Excel text.
\code{\link[=extract_var]{extract_var()}} identifies variables.

\code{\link[=get_blank_cols]{get_blank_cols()}} supports worksheet cleanup.
\code{\link[=filter_second]{filter_second()}} supports comparison cleanup.

Run a single consistency check:
\itemize{
\item \code{\link[=row_group_total_check]{row_group_total_check()}} checks row counts.
\item \code{\link[=row_group_perc_total_check]{row_group_perc_total_check()}} checks row percentages.
\item \code{\link[=col_group_total_check]{col_group_total_check()}} checks column counts.
\item \code{\link[=col_group_perc_total_check]{col_group_perc_total_check()}} checks column percentages.
\item \code{\link[=row_indent_group_total_check]{row_indent_group_total_check()}} checks indented counts.
\item \code{\link[=row_indent_group_perc_total_check]{row_indent_group_perc_total_check()}} checks indented percentages.
}
Each returns status, issue count, all comparisons, and failing comparisons.
Stateful helpers take a session. See \link{offline-workflow} for individual-step
examples. The \code{\link[=calc_cells]{calc_cells()}} help page includes a runnable weighted example.
}

